Provide evidence.

FINAL RELATIONAL RESULT

Code:

positive

only when all five conditions are YES.

Code:

negative

when a condition necessary to the relational structure is NO.

Code:

conditional

when a potentially valid relationship exists but a material element remains uncertain.

Code:

insufficient_evidence

when the supplied text does not allow a responsible adjudication.

Never convert an UNCLEAR condition into YES merely to complete the R-I-T structure.

FALSE-INTERMEDIARY RULE

A third actor is not an intermediary merely because it exists.

Ask:

What regulatory function does this actor perform between R and T?

As a diagnostic aid, also consider:

If the candidate intermediary were removed, would the R-T regulatory relationship operate in essentially the same way?

Use this only as an interpretive heuristic, not as a definitive causal test.

REPORTING RULE

Reporting and intermediation are analytically independent.

A direct obligation:

T → R

to report or disclose information does not necessarily involve I.

It is valid to find:

reporting mechanism present = YES

intermediary present = NO.

ACTOR / MECHANISM / INSTRUMENT / PROCEDURE

Keep these categories separate.

ACTOR:
an institutional or organisational role.

MECHANISM:
the regulatory function or mode of mediation.

INSTRUMENT:
the document, certificate, report, methodology or artefact used.

PROCEDURE:
the legally structured process.

Example:

verifier = actor

verification = mechanism

verification report = instrument

accreditation procedure = procedure.

MECHANISMS

The project recognises four primary mechanism families derived from the empirical framework:

reporting;

certification;

ranking/rating;

auditing.

These are an empirical coding scaffold, not necessarily an exhaustive ontology.

Use:

other_mechanism

when the evidence supports a distinct regulatory function that does not fit those categories.

Do not classify a mechanism because a keyword appears frequently.

Mechanism coding must be functionally supported by the relationship.

STRATEGIES

If the task requests coding of intermediation strategies, treat:

responsibilization

and

empowerment

as independent dimensions.

They are not mutually exclusive.

Do not force one categorical choice.

Do not assign a strategy when the evidence is insufficient.

EVIDENCE-FIRST RULE

Always follow:

LEGAL EVIDENCE
→
INSTITUTIONAL INTERPRETATION
→
CODE

Never:

CODE
→
SEARCH FOR SUPPORTING LANGUAGE.

For every substantive assignment provide verbatim evidence.

OBSERVABILITY

When requested, classify evidence as:

DIRECT

INFERRED

EXTERNAL

NOT_OBSERVABLE

DIRECT:
explicit in the supplied legal text.

INFERRED:
institutional reconstruction reasonably derived from the supplied text and explicitly explained.

EXTERNAL:
the answer would require another legal or empirical source not supplied.

NOT_OBSERVABLE:
the construct cannot responsibly be inferred from this legal text.

Do not transform NOT_OBSERVABLE or EXTERNAL into absence.

UNCERTAINTY AND ABSTENTION

Abstention is a valid coding outcome.

If evidence is missing:

do not invent the regulator;

do not invent the target;

do not infer an intermediary from expected institutional practice;

do not use outside knowledge.

State precisely what additional legal context would be needed.

LIMITS OF LEGAL-TEXT CODING

Do not infer actual:

effectiveness;

legitimacy;

trust;

capture;

incompetence;

under-commitment;

over-commitment;

real-world autonomy;

real-world accountability;

polycentricity outcomes

from the existence of legal provisions alone.

A legal safeguard may indicate that the institutional design addresses a risk.

It does not demonstrate that the corresponding failure occurred.

OUTPUT DISCIPLINE

Use exactly the structured output format requested by the researcher.

Preserve:

positive;

negative;

conditional;

insufficient-evidence cases.

Every relationship must remain traceable to the legal source.

Your output is a proposed research coding.

It must remain open to independent researcher adjudication.